package templates

// laneMark renders the mark for a lane beside its display name (#601).
//
// An unknown or empty token renders NOTHING -- no placeholder box, no reserved
// gap, no broken image. A lane whose mark is not yet sourced is shown as its
// name alone and looks deliberate rather than missing, which is what lets the
// provider marks land later without this being a half-rendered state now.
//
// ACCESSIBILITY: the glyph is aria-hidden and the lane NAME always renders as
// adjacent text, so the accessible name comes from the text and the mark
// contributes nothing to the accessibility tree.
//
// That is deliberate and is the strictest reading of the issue's requirement
// that no row announce a verdict via its lane icon. Labeling the SVG
// "Instrumental Detector" would satisfy the letter of it, but the name is
// already right there in the adjacent cell, so a screen reader would announce
// the lane twice per row. Hiding the decoration and letting the text carry the
// name gives the correct announcement exactly once.
//
// The trade-off this encodes, from the issue: the muted-microphone glyph names
// the detector's VERDICT, while the lane decides "not instrumental" roughly 72%
// of the time. A sighted user reads the glyph as lane identity from its
// repetition and its position beside the name; a screen-reader user never meets
// the drawing at all. Neither is told "no vocals" on a row where the detector
// found vocals.
templ laneMark(token string) {
	switch token {
		case "instrumental-detector":
			@iconInstrumentalDetector()
		case "musixmatch":
			@providerMark("/static/img/lanes/musixmatch.svg")
	}
}

// providerMark renders a vendored third-party brand asset.
//
// Distinct from the authored glyph above in three ways that all follow from
// provenance rather than from taste: it is an <img> to a vendored file rather
// than inline markup (the asset is used unmodified, byte-for-byte as supplied,
// which is what the brand terms require), it carries the chip treatment (a
// fixed-color mark cannot be recolored to suit the theme, so the background is
// adapted to the mark instead), and its path is passed in so every asset
// location sits in the laneMark switch above -- a rebrand or takedown is a
// one-line change there, not a hunt through templates.
//
// aria-hidden with an empty alt for the same reason as the authored glyph: the
// lane name renders as adjacent text and is the accessible name. A non-empty
// alt here would announce the lane twice on every row.
//
// Dimensions are set as attributes so the row does not reflow while the asset
// loads; the CSS sizes it in em to track the surrounding type.
templ providerMark(src string) {
	<img class="mx-lane-mark mx-lane-chip" src={ src } alt="" aria-hidden="true" width="16" height="16"/>
}

// iconInstrumentalDetector: a muted microphone, authored in-repo because the
// detector is the one lane with no company behind it and no brand kit to source
// from. Exact geometry from #601, chosen over three other drafts for legibility
// at 16px and for borrowing the convention instrumental releases already use.
//
// Integer coordinates so it stays sharp at 16px, and currentColor so it themes
// for free in both light and dark rather than needing a second asset.
//
// If this is ever reported as confusing, the issue records the first swap to
// reach for: `note-scan` (a musical note inside viewfinder corners), which
// describes the lane's activity rather than its verdict and was truer on every
// row, but measurably denser at 16px. Reach for that, not a redraw.
templ iconInstrumentalDetector() {
	<svg class="mx-lane-mark" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true">
		<rect x="9" y="3" width="6" height="10" rx="3"></rect>
		<path d="M6 11v1a6 6 0 0 0 12 0v-1"></path>
		<path d="M12 18v3"></path>
		<path d="M4 4 20 20"></path>
	</svg>
}

// laneCell renders a lane's mark and name together as one unit.
//
// A single component rather than the two-line pair repeated at each of the four
// call sites: the mark/name pairing, its spacing, and the no-mark degrade are
// one decision, and duplicating them is how three surfaces drift into looking
// slightly different from the fourth.
templ laneCell(name, mark string) {
	<span class="mx-lane-cell">
		@laneMark(mark)
		<span>{ name }</span>
	</span>
}
